  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 PM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nA shortwave rounding the pinnacle of the central CONUS ridge has\nbeen producing showers across eastern Nebraska today. The\nenvironment has been conducive to heavy rain as moisture has\nbeen advecting from the Gulf over the past 36+ hours. PWAT \nvalues are anomalous for the season at about 2\" which is in the\n99th percentile for late July / early August (NAEFS). \n\nThe northern half of the area continues to record rain this\nafternoon. Rainfall rates of 2-3\" (and one report of 5\") an \nhour were reported around sunrise. With a broad shield of\nprecip, localized areas of heavy rain are becoming less common.\nStill, two gauges just south of Plainview, NE (Antelope County)\nhave reported over 5\" since midnight with 2-3\" common in the \nrest of the county over the same time period. We're finally \ngetting some eastward motion with this area, allowing for some \nclearing over the wettest spots. This should allow for the\ndevelopment of notable instability by this evening. Still, the \ncloud cover encouraged me to lower forecast highs in most \nlocations by a couple of degrees today.",
